  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lucas's reply concerns a given first machine and an enlargement of it. The target treats only PA and PA + Con(PA). It gives an instance, not a statement that one can pass from a given suitable machine to a second one."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The sentence gained is Con(PA), not the first machine's Gödelian formula G(PA). Chalmers distinguishes the two: the target states the PA instance of (c), unprovability of consistency, but states neither (a), unprovability of G(PA), nor (b), PA's ability to prove the conditional from consistency to G(PA). It does not identify a Gödel sentence or state its relation to consistency."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lucas's rejoinder is that Gödel's procedure applies afresh to the second machine, yielding a formula of its own that it cannot produce as true. The target stops at the second theory's proof of the first theory's consistency. It states no incompleteness result for the second theory."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The target asserts outright that every theorem of PA + Con(PA) is true in the standard natural numbers. Lucas says the second machine can produce the previously missed formula, not that all its outputs are true. Chalmers considers soundness as a hypothesis on a system capturing our reasoning, alongside the possibility of unsoundness; he does not conclude that an enlarged machine is sound."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The target has no notion of a system capturing someone's reasoning or of what that person can recognize as true. In particular, the external assertion of soundness does not address Chalmers's warning that we may be unable to see our own system's consistency and thus lack grounds for recognizing its Gödel sentence as true."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The implementation of the enlargement is more specific in one respect and more abstract in another: the target adjoins the missed sentence as an axiom, whereas Lucas only requires that the new machine can produce it. It represents machinehood by the existence of recursively enumerable axioms with exactly the enlarged theory's theorems, rather than describing a machine or its operations."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target captures one feature of the reply: a mechanically presentable extension proves a sentence its predecessor did not. It substitutes PA's consistency sentence for the Gödelian formula and omits Lucas's decisive next step, the new limitation of the enlarged machine."
}
